Subject: [PATCH] yttrium: render LINEAR colour targets next to depth through an OPTIMAL shadow

Shared colour images, among them every D3D9 and DXGI back buffer, are
LINEAR, and the venus2 draw path binds them directly as colour attachments.
NVIDIA's driver (610.57.04, RTX 5090) sets no
VK_FORMAT_FEATURE_COLOR_ATTACHMENT_BIT in linearTilingFeatures for any
format, although vkGetPhysicalDeviceImageFormatProperties accepts the usage.
Rendering into such an image alone happens to work, which is why dwm and the
probes run. A render pass that also has a depth attachment does not: the
first draw makes the GPU reject the method (host Xid 69, class 0xce97,
DRAW_INDEX_BUFFER_BEGIN_END_B or DRAW_VERTEX_ARRAY_BEGIN_END_B, ErrorCode
0x9c), the channel is torn down and the guest application hangs. The DirectX
SDK's BasicHLSL hits it on its first draw, and so does any application that
draws with a depth buffer into its back buffer. RADV and ANV advertise linear
colour attachments and are unaffected.

When the renderer lacks linear COLOR_ATTACHMENT support for the format and
the render pass has a depth attachment, the pipeline's colour view now names
an OPTIMAL "render shadow" image of the same format, extent, levels and
layers, created once per resource and retired with it. The draw copies the
target level from the LINEAR image into the shadow before vkCmdBeginRenderPass
and back after vkCmdEndRenderPass, so the LINEAR image stays the copy that
presentation, sharing and readback see. Such draws are not deferred into
the render-pass batch, whose replay has no place for the copies. Draws without
depth, feedback-loop attachments and renderers with linear colour attachment
support are unchanged.

Evidence: docs/xid69.md, docs/evidence/xid69/ (host-tests/lindepth.c and
its output reproduce the Xid with no VM).
Origin: vmlab-win3d xid69 worker.
